              <h1 style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">Every Address-Solution Vendor Scores Itself 5/5.<br>Here&rsquo;s the Scorecard They Didn&rsquo;t Write.</h1>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">I have read a lot of vendor decks over thirty years, and address-solution decks have a tell: <strong>every one of them scores itself five out of five.</strong></p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The postal-verification platform is &ldquo;fully ISO 20022 ready.&rdquo; The LLM structurer is &ldquo;99% accurate.&rdquo; The free network utility is &ldquo;production-grade.&rdquo; Each claim is defensible inside the vendor&rsquo;s own frame of reference &mdash; and useless to you, because none of the frames is yours: a regulated payment operation with a fixed deadline, an examiner who will ask why, and a sanctions-screening layer that inherits whatever your parser produces.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">So how do you actually evaluate an ISO 20022 address solution when every option in front of you claims to be perfect? You need a scorecard the vendors did not write.</p>

              <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
