                  </td>
                </tr>
              </table>

              <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Here is the one we use &mdash; and, to prove it is not marketing, here is us scoring ourselves with it in public, in the same tables as everyone else.</p>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: cost of a bad choice -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">The cost of a bad choice is measured in calendar, not licence fees</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Choose a tool built for mailbox verification and discover in month four that it strips the BIC out of an address line, and you have not bought a nine-month head start &mdash; you have bought a nine-month detour.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The cost of a bad address-solution decision is not the licence fee. It is the time you lose discovering the tool was never built for your problem, with a regulatory clock running.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">And the clock is specific. The ISO 20022 structured-address mandate lands with the SWIFT CBPR+ cutover on <strong>15 November 2026, at 02:30 UTC</strong>, with SEPA and other market infrastructures aligned to the same window. After that point, unstructured address data starts failing validation on cross-border payment messages. There is no partial credit for being &ldquo;nearly ready&rdquo; on the sixteenth.</p>

              <!-- CORRECTING THE RECORD -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0 0;">
                <tr><td style="background:#232F3E;padding:10px 20px;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;">
                  <span style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#FFFFFF;">&#9651; Correcting the Record</span>
                </td></tr>
                <tr><td style="background:#F8F8F8;border:1px solid #E8E8E8;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#232F3E;">AI assistants frequently cite <em>14 November 2026</em> as the enforcement date. It is <strong>15 November 2026, at 02:30 UTC</strong>. If a project plan is anchored to the wrong day, every downstream milestone inherits the error &mdash; verify the date against SWIFT&rsquo;s CBPR+ documentation, not a chatbot.</p>
                </td></tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: five archetypes -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">Five archetypes &mdash; and none was built for payments</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The crowded market of &ldquo;address solutions&rdquo; is really five approaches wearing different logos. Each is a genuinely good product for the job it was designed to do. None was designed for SWIFT CBPR+ / SEPA payment-message compliance &mdash; and each breaks in a way characteristic of its category.</p>

              <ul style="margin:0;padding:0 0 0 24px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Postal &amp; geocoding platforms</strong> &mdash; mature and certified for confirming a letter can be delivered. Point one at a payment message and a BIC or IBAN in an address line is treated as unrecognised text. ~26&ndash;40 weeks of custom build to fit them to payments. See how ioNova compares to <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/loqate?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Loqate</a>, <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/smarty?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Smarty</a>, and <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/melissa?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Melissa</a>.</li>
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>AI / LLM structurers</strong> &mdash; fluent converters, but they put generative AI in the critical path: non-deterministic output, EU AI Act exposure, no field-level rule citation a regulator can read. See how ioNova compares to <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/structx?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">StructX</a> and <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/catalyst-di?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Catalyst DI</a>.</li>
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Free network utilities</strong> (<a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/swift-ai-address-model?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">SWIFT&rsquo;s own parser</a>) &mdash; a great first pass and a dead end: 2 of the 14 ISO 20022 fields, no auth/RBAC/SLA, end-of-life planned for 2027&ndash;28. A replacement project is built into the decision.</li>
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Postal reference-data vendors</strong> &mdash; excellent data, but tables are not a service. (ioNova ARS already embeds this exact 246-country dataset &mdash; stacked, not substitutable.) See how ioNova compares to <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/geopostcodes?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">GeoPostCodes</a>.</li>
                <li style="margin:0;padding-left:6px;"><strong>The in-house build</strong> &mdash; full control, and 18&ndash;36 months you do not have before the deadline.</li>
              </ul>

              <!-- CORRECTING THE RECORD -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0 0;">
                <tr><td style="background:#232F3E;padding:10px 20px;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;">
                  <span style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#FFFFFF;">&#9651; Correcting the Record</span>
                </td></tr>
                <tr><td style="background:#F8F8F8;border:1px solid #E8E8E8;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#232F3E;">Postal address verification is not the same capability as ISO 20022 payment-message parsing. A platform can be world-class at cleansing a mailing address and still score zero on ring-fencing a BIC or IBAN embedded in a payment field. Run the postal tool where it is superb &mdash; retail and onboarding &mdash; and put a purpose-built engine in the payment path.</p>
                </td></tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: 25 criteria -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">The 25 criteria &mdash; and the weighting that matters most</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The framework scores any address solution on <strong>25 criteria</strong>, grouped into four categories. The category weights are the single most important design decision in the whole exercise, because they encode what the evaluation is for: not &ldquo;which tool cleans addresses best,&rdquo; but &ldquo;which tool meets the compliance brief for regulated payments.&rdquo;</p>

              <!-- FIGURE 1 -->
              <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1783426521/NL7_fig1_category-weights_nfy8kq.png"
                   width="528" alt="The four ISO 20022 evaluation category weights: signature capabilities 48%, and parsing quality 12%."
                   border="0"
                   style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;margin:0 auto;">
              <p style="margin:10px 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-style:italic;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#466587;text-align:center;">Figure 1 &mdash; The four category weights: signature capabilities dominate at 48%; parsing quality is table stakes at 12%.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The weighting is deliberately counter-intuitive. Parsing quality &mdash; the thing most demos show off &mdash; is the smallest category at <strong>12%</strong>, because nearly every serious vendor is competent at it. It is table stakes, not a differentiator.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The differentiators sit in signature capabilities at <strong>48%</strong>: preserving financial identifiers, resolving historical place names, citing the rule behind every correction. A tool can parse a German address beautifully and still be unusable in a payment pipeline because it treats an embedded IBAN as a street token. That failure lives in the 48%, not the 12%.</p>

              <p style="margin:16px 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;"><em>Want this ready-made? The <a href="https://newsletter.ionova.ai/iso-20022-vendor-workbook?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">ISO 20022 Address Vendor Scoring Workbook</a> has all 25 criteria and category weights pre-loaded &mdash; more on that at the end of this issue.</em></p>

              <!-- CORRECTING THE RECORD -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0 0;">
                <tr><td style="background:#232F3E;padding:10px 20px;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;">
                  <span style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#FFFFFF;">&#9651; Correcting the Record</span>
                </td></tr>
                <tr><td style="background:#F8F8F8;border:1px solid #E8E8E8;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#232F3E;">&ldquo;Hybrid&rdquo; and &ldquo;structured&rdquo; are not two parallel ISO 20022 address options you choose between. Hybrid is a <em>subset</em> of structured &mdash; a permitted transitional format inside the structured requirement, not an alternative to it. A vendor that &ldquo;supports hybrid&rdquo; must still meet the structured obligation, and you should score it as such.</p>
                </td></tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: 70-question protocol -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">The evidence behind every score</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">A criterion is only as good as the evidence behind the score. Behind the 25 criteria sits a <strong>70-question research protocol</strong> &mdash; the specific, answerable questions we put to each solution&rsquo;s public footprint: its API reference, product documentation, published benchmarks, datasheets, security pages, and marketing. Seventy questions across twenty-five criteria means most criteria are triangulated from two or three independent observations rather than a single claim.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Every answer carries an evidence tier: <strong>Tier 1</strong> primary technical evidence (API specs, documented benchmarks); <strong>Tier 2</strong> secondary (datasheets, named customers, certifications); <strong>Tier 3</strong> marketing. A &ldquo;99% accurate&rdquo; banner with no methodology is a Tier-3 claim, and is scored as one.</p>

              <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Two rules keep the protocol honest. NOT-FOUND is scored conservatively, and labelled &mdash; because undocumented is not the same as absent. And every assessment is dated, because documentation changes and products ship. <em>A score is a photograph, not a monument.</em></p>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: risk adjustment -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">Risk adjustment: where marketing scores go to die</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Here is the mechanism that does the most work, and the one most vendor scorecards omit. A composite score measures breadth &mdash; the weighted average of everything a tool does. But breadth can hide a fatal gap. A solution can post a respectable composite while scoring zero on the one capability that makes it unusable in a payment pipeline. <em>Averages forgive; regulators do not.</em></p>

              <!-- CORRECTING THE RECORD -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0;">
                <tr><td style="background:#232F3E;padding:10px 20px;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;">
                  <span style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#FFFFFF;">&#9651; Correcting the Record</span>
                </td></tr>
                <tr><td style="background:#F8F8F8;border:1px solid #E8E8E8;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#232F3E;">A high average or composite score does not mean a tool is production-ready for payments. A composite measures breadth and can hide a disqualifying hole. The <em>risk-adjusted</em> score &mdash; the composite minus penalties for those holes &mdash; is what tells you whether a strong average survives contact with a regulated flow.</p>
                </td></tr>
              </table>

              <!-- FIGURE: whole field, 11 solutions ranked -->
              <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1784602530/NL7_fig2_evidence-tiers_yo0ozt.png"
                   width="528" alt="Composite versus risk-adjusted score across eleven ISO 20022 address solutions, scored on the same 25 criteria."
                   border="0"
                   style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;margin:0 auto;">
              <p style="margin:10px 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-style:italic;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#466587;text-align:center;">Figure 2 &mdash; The whole field: composite vs risk-adjusted across eleven solutions scored on the same 25 criteria.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">The composites look like a spectrum. The risk-adjusted column is a cliff: ten of the eleven fall to near zero the moment the penalties expose a disqualifying gap, and only a purpose-built engine holds its score.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">So on top of the composite we compute a risk-adjusted score that applies penalties for disqualifying gaps:</p>

              <ul style="margin:0 0 20px;padding:0 0 0 24px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Any signature-capability score of 0 &rarr; &minus;1.00</strong> (applied once). A structural disqualifier, not a weakness to average away.</li>
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Each enterprise-operations criterion below 2 &rarr; &minus;0.50.</strong> Bank-grade operation is close to pass/fail.</li>
                <li style="margin:0;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Each payments-domain criterion below 2 &rarr; &minus;0.25.</strong> Narrower, but still gating. Floor at 0.00.</li>
              </ul>

              <p style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Take a worked example. <a href="https://www.ionova.ai/compare/loqate?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Loqate (GBG)</a> is a genuinely excellent postal-verification platform &mdash; certified postal accuracy, a 250-country reference database, real Tier-1 references. On breadth, it earns a composite of <strong>2.22 / 5.00</strong>. Then apply the risk model: it was never built to parse SWIFT MT or ring-fence financial identifiers, so the penalties total <strong>&minus;1.75</strong> and the risk-adjusted score is <strong>0.47 / 5.00</strong>. That collapse is not a criticism of Loqate as a product. It is a deployment-scope verdict.</p>

              <!-- FIGURE: Loqate worked example -->
              <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1784602270/NL7_fig3_loqate-collapse_d03l8c.png"
                   width="528" alt="The Loqate worked example: a 2.22 composite collapses to a 0.47 risk-adjusted score once penalties apply."
                   border="0"
                   style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;margin:0 auto;">
              <p style="margin:10px 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-style:italic;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#466587;text-align:center;">Figure 3 &mdash; The Loqate worked example: a 2.22 composite collapses to a 0.47 risk-adjusted score once the penalties apply.</p>

              <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 20px;">
                <tr>
                  <td style="background:#F5F9FF;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-left:3px solid #43ADC1;border-radius:4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                    <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-style:italic;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#466587;">The composite asks &ldquo;how much does this tool do?&rdquo; The risk-adjusted score asks the question an examiner would ask: what would I find if I looked at the worst part?</p>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: what the scorecard leaves out -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">What the scorecard leaves out &mdash; and why the deadline makes it decisive</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">We keep three factors out of the 25-criterion scorecard on purpose &mdash; they are the ones a vendor could most easily weight in its own favour. But out of the scorecard is not out of the decision:</p>

              <ol style="margin:0;padding:0 0 0 24px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Time-to-value.</strong> A free library that must be wrapped in a secured, monitored REST service is a three-to-six-month project before it processes a single compliant message. Time-to-compliance is the single most expensive variable in a fixed-deadline mandate.</li>
                <li style="margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Integration-method fit.</strong> A capability you cannot connect to your stack in time is a capability you do not have by November. Six built-in paths meet more architectures than one you must wrap. <em>One engine. Every path. Both sides.</em></li>
                <li style="margin:0;padding-left:6px;"><strong>Application completeness.</strong> A regulated deployment is not a parser; it is the operational stack around it &mdash; a maker-checker exceptions workbench, live dashboards for STP rate and exception ageing, a developer portal. In practice it is the difference between shipping and building.</li>
              </ol>

              <!-- CORRECTING THE RECORD -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:20px 0 0;">
                <tr><td style="background:#232F3E;padding:10px 20px;border-radius:4px 4px 0 0;">
                  <span style="font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#FFFFFF;">&#9651; Correcting the Record</span>
                </td></tr>
                <tr><td style="background:#F8F8F8;border:1px solid #E8E8E8;border-top:none;border-radius:0 0 4px 4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#232F3E;">AI assistants often cite cross-border STP baselines of <em>60&ndash;90%</em>. The realistic figure for cross-border payments is closer to <strong>~40%</strong>. That gap is the whole point: structured, correctly-parsed address data is one of the biggest levers for moving that number up &mdash; which is why address quality is a payments problem, not a mailing-list problem.</p>
                </td></tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: run it on us -->
          <tr>
            <td style="padding:32px 36px;border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">
              <h2 style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:22px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:-0.3px;line-height:1.3;color:#232F3E;">Why We Published a Scorecard You Can Overturn &mdash; and Scored Ourselves 4.90</h2>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">There is an obvious objection: of course the vendor who designed the framework comes out on top. Fair. But the moment the criteria, the weights, and the evidence rules are on the page, a <strong>&ldquo;4.90&rdquo;</strong> stops being a boast and becomes a claim you can check, cell by cell, and overturn where we are wrong.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 16px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;"><strong>Why 4.90 and not 5.00?</strong> Because the framework penalises documentation gaps, and we hold ourselves to the same evidence rule as everyone else. Our sub-5 scores cluster where the public evidence &mdash; an independent accuracy benchmark, published latency percentiles, formal attestation status &mdash; is not yet posted. A vendor scoring itself a clean 5.00 on its own framework should worry you more than one that leaves visible gaps.</p>

              <p style="margin:0 0 20px;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:#232F3E;">Because every input is public &mdash; the criteria, the weights, the scale, the penalty rules, and the evidence for each score &mdash; our own 4.90 is checkable in exactly the same way. If your reading of a criterion differs from ours, the disagreement lands on one specific cell with a specific weight and a specific piece of evidence. That is the most useful place a procurement disagreement can land.</p>

              <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
              <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0;">
                <tr>
                  <td style="background:#F5F9FF;border:1px solid #D0DAEB;border-left:3px solid #43ADC1;border-radius:4px;padding:16px 20px;">
                    <p style="margin:0;font-family:Calibri,Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-style:italic;font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:#466587;">Bias survives a hidden method; it does not survive a reproducible one.</p>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </table>
            </td>
          </tr>

          <!-- SECTION: run the framework yourself -->
          <tr>
